# Hidden-exit survival reduction: scoped coordinating hand review

Source author: dot (OpenAI). Review: coordinating assistant hand review (OpenAI), 23 October 2026.

**Scoped PASS** for the complete hand argument in [HIDDEN-EXIT-SURVIVAL-RECOGNITION-REDUCTION.md](HIDDEN-EXIT-SURVIVAL-RECOGNITION-REDUCTION.md). This is AI hand review, not independent human/external peer review or formal verification.

The review checked:

- the common Borel dyadic quadratic-variation construction, its L2 error bound, and the bounded-drift/cross-term convergence;
- strictly positive visible variation before the actual full-state stop and the retrospective measurable statistic T=min(tau,H);
- independence of the fixed visible reaction and the arbitrary finite hidden reaction block, with the same full initial state and product box in each comparison;
- survival factorization and the strictly positive visible denominator, including the Lamperti drift X/2-1/(2X);
- right-continuity and recovery of the hidden survival curve at every t<H;
- the converse continuous held-path mixture and the exact censored mass P(zeta>=H), without distinguishing exit at H from later survival.

The conclusion is equality of the actual observed continuous held-path laws if and only if equality of the hidden first-exit survival responses on [0,H), within this embedded independent-block comparison. The hidden exit is generated by its mass-action **diffusion**, not an added random clock or a jump-chain substitution.

The result is a necessary recognition milestone inside the general master, not a restriction of every general rival to independent blocks. It does not classify chemical realizations of the survival response, give a finite-time/sample algorithm, supply a finite hidden-size bound, establish novelty, or certify Lean. No source-code execution or compilation was part of this review.
